course: dictionary
lesson: 329
title: An old word for tiny life
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old-fashioned. People used them long ago, and you rarely hear them now. Animalcule is one of these. It sounds like it might mean a small animal, and that idea is close: it is a tiny living thing that you can only see with a microscope.
explain: «Antonie van Leeuwenhoek first saw tiny living things in water, which he called "animalcules", with homemade lenses in the 1670s.» Read the clues around the word: tiny, living, in water, and seen through lenses. Those clues tell you what an animalcule is, even before you look it up.
explain: Today scientists use other words for the same idea, such as microorganisms or microbes. Try swapping one in: "Leeuwenhoek saw tiny living things in water, which he called microbes." It still makes sense, so you have understood the meaning.
fact: Animalcule is an old-fashioned word for a tiny living thing.
fact: You need a microscope to see an animalcule.
fact: "Animalcules" was the name Antonie van Leeuwenhoek gave to the tiny living things he saw in water in the 1670s.
fact: Today scientists say "microorganisms" or "microbes" for these tiny living things.
fact: Some animalcules are single-celled creatures, which means each one is made of just one cell.
exercise: In old science, what is an animalcule? | A large farm animal | A kind of water boat | A type of glass window | *A tiny living thing that can only be seen with a microscope
exercise: Find and copy the word from «Antonie van Leeuwenhoek first saw tiny living things in water» that tells you the things were very small. | water | living | *tiny | Antonie
exercise: Today scientists use another word for animalcules. Which one? | mammoths | castles | *microbes | glassware
test: What does the word «animalcule» tell us about the things Leeuwenhoek saw? | They were made of metal | *They were tiny living things | They were huge machines | They were sweets
test: Which word is old-fashioned, so people rarely use it today? | microbe | microorganism | *animalcule | microscope
exam: Using the context clues, what does «animalcule» most likely mean? | A piece of coloured glass | A big, fierce animal | A kind of cloud | *A tiny living thing seen only with a microscope
